        "says": "An operation passed to a parallel stream must be thread-safe.",
        "basis": [
          "example:13-lambda-expressions/README_4.1.4.md:14"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "thread-safety failures are not deterministic, and a practice may not depend on scheduling; audit practises the side-effect-free lambdas that make it safe"
      },
      {
        "id": "peek-skipped",
        "says": "A stream may skip peek entirely, so peek is for debugging and never for work that must happen.",
        "basis": [
          "example:13-lambda-expressions/README_4.1.4.md:15"
        ],
        "exercise": "audit",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "readable-not-short",
        "says": "The aim is code that reveals intent to its readers, not code that is merely shorter.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the guiding idea behind every practice on this page rather than a checkable rule of its own"
      },
      {
        "id": "short-and-formatted",
        "says": "A lambda stays one or two lines, and a multi-statement lambda is laid out like a method body.",
        "basis": [
          "example:13-lambda-expressions/README_4.1.4.md:6",
          "example:13-lambda-expressions/README_4.1.4.md:8"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "length and layout are style, invisible to a test; eligible-items and totals practise the extraction that keeps lambdas short"
      },
      {
        "id": "var-parameters",
        "says": "Since Java 11, var in lambda parameters allows annotations or final without writing the type.",
